(3726853, 514344, 74979, '<td valign="top">really great Doc! So creative!  And congrats on the RED!</td>', 'really great Doc! So creative!  And congrats on the RED!', '2007-06-01 23:11:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(3729112, 518314, 31637, '<td valign="top">nice inverse tornado mechanism. fight the gravity baby, nice placing!</td>', 'nice inverse tornado mechanism. fight the gravity baby, nice placing!', '2007-06-02 15:01:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(3731431, 517845, 63269, '<td valign="top">I''ve seen lots of chess piece pictures, but THIS is neat. The subtle lighting and the green glow adds a very interesting, almost menacing atmosphere. I love it! The tones are wonderful, and the grain is also kind of neat, though it might be cool to see it without the grain to... Was it added? EXCELLENT shot! :)</td>', 'I''ve seen lots of chess piece pictures, but THIS is neat. The subtle lighting and the green glow adds a very interesting, almost menacing atmosphere. I love it! The tones are wonderful, and the grain is also kind of neat, though it might be cool to see it without the grain to... Was it added? EXCELLENT shot! :)', '2007-06-03 10:29:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(3733209, 518993, 42567, '<td valign="top">wow, this is really cool.  Great eye to see this one!</td>', 'wow, this is really cool.  Great eye to see this one!', '2007-06-03 18:51:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(3733735, 518701, 74979, '<td valign="top">really great~ the variety of textures, angles &amp; lighting make this a very interesting capture</td>', 'really great~ the variety of textures, angles & lighting make this a very interesting capture', '2007-06-03 21:19:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(3734044, 514188, 15266, '<td valign="top">Horray and grats on the ribbon! You did it, dude!  This is an awesome shot and the processing was spot on.  Now we''re all in trouble!   :D</td>', 'Horray and grats on the ribbon! You did it, dude!  This is an awesome shot and the processing was spot on.  Now we''re all in trouble!   :D', '2007-06-03 22:55:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(3735889, 517739, 45475, '<td valign="top">Gosh, you do these so well. An inspiration for me to go out more into the garden. Love it.</td>', 'Gosh, you do these so well. An inspiration for me to go out more into the garden. Love it.', '2007-06-04 06:23:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(3736899, 518396, 63269, '<td valign="top">I love this! This really does remind the viewer of air for some reason.. beautiful photo!</td>', 'I love this! This really does remind the viewer of air for some reason.. beautiful photo!', '2007-06-04 13:07:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(3737442, 518235, 77968, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by quiche:</b></div><hr/><i> Would be more interesting if you cropped out the whole right side of the photo so that the focal point was on the parts of the blue-frosted cupcake and sprinkles that are in sharp focus. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Thanks for the offer, however the scope of the project being "Shapes" includes the injection-molded circles, half domes, and geometry of the plastic container. Shallow depth of field in a macro piece precludes the entire frame from being in sharp focus. By limiting the shot to only those in-focus pieces, I doubt that would make an uninteresting shot such as this....any more interesting??</td>', 'Originally posted by quiche: Would be more interesting if you cropped out the whole right side of the photo so that the focal point was on the parts of the blue-frosted cupcake and sprinkles that are in sharp focus.  Thanks for the offer, however the scope of the project being "Shapes" includes the injection-molded circles, half domes, and geometry of the plastic container. Shallow depth of field in a macro piece precludes the entire frame from being in sharp focus. By limiting the shot to only those in-focus pieces, I doubt that would make an uninteresting shot such as this....any more interesting??', '2007-06-04 15:07:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(3737584, 518235, 77968, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by jfharden:</b></div><hr/><i> The colors are pretty and if close down the aperture the whole thing will be in focus. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>f32 perhaps? This is a macro taken with the Canon PowerShot A95. It is optically not possible to hyperfocus.</td>', 'Originally posted by jfharden: The colors are pretty and if close down the aperture the whole thing will be in focus.  f32 perhaps? This is a macro taken with the Canon PowerShot A95. It is optically not possible to hyperfocus.', '2007-06-04 15:31:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(3737612, 518235, 77968, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Darth_Vader:</b></div><hr/><i> Nice colors but not particularly interesting </i></td></tr></table> <br/>I was going for the brilliant color contrasts, shallow depth of field focal area, compositional balancing of right and left with subtle shape perspective in the center, contrast of textures, and (in keeping with the theme of the exercise) including as many "Shapes" as possible. Not.......interesting enough?</td>', 'Originally posted by Darth_Vader: Nice colors but not particularly interesting  I was going for the brilliant color contrasts, shallow depth of field focal area, compositional balancing of right and left with subtle shape perspective in the center, contrast of textures, and (in keeping with the theme of the exercise) including as many "Shapes" as possible. Not.......interesting enough?', '2007-06-04 15:38:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(3738520, 514344, 25166, '<td valign="top">Great shot. I the title a NIN reference?<a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=437852" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25166/thumb/437852.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25166/thumb/437852.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/25166/thumb/437852.jpg"/></a><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-06-04 18:47:11.</i></td>', 'Great shot. I the title a NIN reference?', '2007-06-04 19:45:49.000000', '2007-06-04 19:47:11.000000', 0),
(3738725, 517613, 45475, '<td valign="top">Hey, nice work Aimee! Great shapes and the colour is wonderful. Backlighting table, here I come! ;D</td>', 'Hey, nice work Aimee! Great shapes and the colour is wonderful. Backlighting table, here I come! ;D', '2007-06-04 20:30:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(3740517, 515802, 66515, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>A great idea that unfortunately needed something more to give it that "Wow" factor for the voters. The angle is probably where I would start. Getting a lower angle would definitely help.<br/> <br/>Even with this type of shot, try to think in terms of what your main object of focus is. There is a lot going on in this picture and the viewer doesn''t know what you want them to look at. A lot angle with the wide aperture you used would draw the eye to the items in focus. It would then start straying away from the central area and start exploring. It would also create a feeling of depth. This composition is unfortunately a little two dimensional. <br/> <br/>Desaturating the colors might have also worked against you. You have a lot of vibrant colors in this shot. Subduing them really takes away alot of the impact I think. Just as a thought (i.e. something to try some time). Try to organize these by color. Give the shot an overall them. <br/> <br/>I would really be curious how this would turn out if you give some of these things a try. It might be worth a second try I think.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club! A great idea that unfortunately needed something more to give it that "Wow" factor for the voters. The angle is probably where I would start. Getting a lower angle would definitely help. Even with this type of shot, try to think in terms of what your main object of focus is. There is a lot going on in this picture and the viewer doesn''t know what you want them to look at. A lot angle with the wide aperture you used would draw the eye to the items in focus. It would then start straying away from the central area and start exploring. It would also create a feeling of depth. This composition is unfortunately a little two dimensional.  Desaturating the colors might have also worked against you. You have a lot of vibrant colors in this shot. Subduing them really takes away alot of the impact I think. Just as a thought (i.e. something to try some time). Try to organize these by color. Give the shot an overall them.  I would really be curious how this would turn out if you give some of these things a try. It might be worth a second try I think.', '2007-06-05 03:02:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(3740546, 517792, 66515, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>Interesting approach to the challenge. The effect you were going for works fairly well. Lighting is pretty decent and composition is okay. To cut to the chase, the technicals are fine here. Perhaps a little more depth portrayed in the shot would be nice but otherwise its alright.<br/> <br/>Unfortunately with this shot, there''s that wow factor missing. As mentioned in the previous comments, more candles might help. Perhaps moving the candles around and spacing them out and placing them at varying distances from the camera may help. You''ve composed using the rule of thirds which is fine. Try it also in the third dimension as well. You could also try different levels for variety. (A black hockey puck as a stand might allow the change in levels without being noticable in the scene)<br/> <br/>Previous commenters mentioned changing the background. Maybe but I honestly like it the way it is. Your eye is drawn to the star patterns. <br/> <br/>Potentially you could have closed your aperture a fair bit and used a shorter exposure time to try to get just the stars. (Just an idea though.)<br/> <br/>Overall, I like the fact you were trying to be creative with your topic. Keep the ideas coming!</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club! Interesting approach to the challenge. The effect you were going for works fairly well. Lighting is pretty decent and composition is okay. To cut to the chase, the technicals are fine here. Perhaps a little more depth portrayed in the shot would be nice but otherwise its alright. Unfortunately with this shot, there''s that wow factor missing. As mentioned in the previous comments, more candles might help. Perhaps moving the candles around and spacing them out and placing them at varying distances from the camera may help. You''ve composed using the rule of thirds which is fine. Try it also in the third dimension as well. You could also try different levels for variety. (A black hockey puck as a stand might allow the change in levels without being noticable in the scene) Previous commenters mentioned changing the background. Maybe but I honestly like it the way it is. Your eye is drawn to the star patterns.  Potentially you could have closed your aperture a fair bit and used a shorter exposure time to try to get just the stars. (Just an idea though.) Overall, I like the fact you were trying to be creative with your topic. Keep the ideas coming!', '2007-06-05 03:13:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(3740846, 518304, 76335, '<td valign="top">I do like this, but personally would like to have seen more face and less umbrella -- or complete silhouette and a more interesting background.</td>', 'I do like this, but personally would like to have seen more face and less umbrella -- or complete silhouette and a more interesting background.', '2007-06-05 08:30:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(3741523, 517361, 29011, '<td valign="top">Congratulations Tim :)<br/>Really like your wider angle look. It''s all about the shapes and fun setting it up. Though I can imagine it isn''t as easy as it looks to get it just as you want it. wtg!</td>', 'Congratulations Tim :)Really like your wider angle look. It''s all about the shapes and fun setting it up. Though I can imagine it isn''t as easy as it looks to get it just as you want it. wtg!', '2007-06-05 12:16:48.000000', NULL, 0),
